// ============================================================================
// Hunt: Rackspace Spot breach - leaked-credential Kubernetes Secrets theft
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1078 (Valid Accounts), T1610 (Deploy Container),
//   T1552.001 (Unsecured Credentials: Credentials in Files),
//   T1552.007 (Unsecured Credentials: Container API),
//   T1083 (File and Directory Discovery)
// Data: network events (DeviceNetworkEvents), process events
//   (DeviceProcessEvents), file events (DeviceFileEvents)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-22-2026.html
// Logic: On November 17, 2026, between about 00:20 and 22:00 UTC, an
// unauthorized party used leaked credentials to access Rackspace Spot,
// the company's managed Kubernetes platform, acting as legitimate users
// in some customer organizations. The attackers created pods in customer
// clusters, read Kubernetes Secrets, and listed nodes and volumes.
// Kubernetes Secrets hold passwords, authentication tokens and API keys
// used to reach databases, cloud services and other systems, so any
// Secret read on November 17 stays exposed until it is rotated.
// Rackspace learned of the intrusion on November 18 from a customer
// report, revoked affected tokens and sessions, removed the unauthorized
// pods, rotated keys, and announced full service restoration on
// November 21. It shared these indicators of compromise: the IP address
// 23.224.79.106, the range 23.224.0.0/15, and suspicious Kubernetes pod
// and object prefixes i1, glm- and probe. How the credentials leaked in
// the first place has not been disclosed, the number of affected
// customers has not been disclosed, and Rackspace has not said whether
// customer data was stolen. The intrusion window is the priority lookback
// (November 17, 00:20-22:00 UTC), but the queries below run 30 days so
// they also catch staging or follow-on activity - the real exposure
// window is wider than the 22 hours of access, because a Secret read on
// November 17 remains valid until rotated. No other IoCs have been
// published - these queries hunt the published indicators plus the
// behaviour (pod creation, Secret reads, node/volume listing) and invent
// no hashes, domains or attacker IPs. Variant 1 hunts egress to the
// published IP and range. Variant 2 hunts kubectl pod creation using the
// published prefixes. Variant 3 hunts kubectl reads of Kubernetes
// Secrets. Variant 4 hunts kubectl or kubelet spawning shells and
// downloaders after a pod is created. Variant 5 hunts non-kubectl
// processes reading kubeconfig or service-account token files.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: egress to the published Rackspace Spot IoC range --
// Rackspace published 23.224.79.106 and the range 23.224.0.0/15
// (23.224.0.0 - 23.225.255.255). Any egress from your fleet to that
// range is worth triaging immediately - legitimate traffic to it should
// be near zero. The intrusion window was November 17, 00:20-22:00 UTC;
// run the 30-day lookback first, then narrow to that window.
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where ipv4_is_in_range(RemoteIP, "23.224.0.0/15")
    or RemoteIP == "23.224.79.106"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, InitiatingProcessAccountName,
          InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
          RemoteIP, RemotePort, RemoteUrl
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: kubectl creating pods with the published prefixes --
// The attackers created pods in customer clusters; Rackspace flagged
// the pod/object prefixes i1, glm- and probe. Hunt kubectl pod-creation
// commands carrying those prefixes. Note the "probe" prefix is noisy -
// liveness/readiness probes mention it legitimately - so triage hits on
// the full pod name (i1-* and glm-* are far more specific) and baseline
// your own naming conventions before alerting on "probe" alone.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName =~ "kubectl.exe" or FileName =~ "kubectl"
| where ProcessCommandLine has "create" or ProcessCommandLine has "run"
    or ProcessCommandLine has "apply"
| where ProcessCommandLine has "i1-" or ProcessCommandLine has "glm-"
    or ProcessCommandLine has "probe"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, AccountName, ProcessCommandLine,
          InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3: kubectl reading Kubernetes Secrets --
// The attackers read Kubernetes Secrets from affected clusters. Hunt
// kubectl secret reads and flag any that fall outside a known change
// window, come from an unusual account or host, or target secrets in
// production namespaces. A single read of a production secret by an
// account that does not normally touch it is worth triaging - the
// Secrets hold database passwords, cloud API keys and tokens.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName =~ "kubectl.exe" or FileName =~ "kubectl"
| where ProcessCommandLine has "secret" or ProcessCommandLine has "secrets"
| where ProcessCommandLine has "get" or ProcessCommandLine has "describe"
    or ProcessCommandLine has "edit"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, AccountName, ProcessCommandLine,
          InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 4: kubectl or kubelet spawning shells/downloaders --
// After creating a pod, post-exploitation tooling on the node shows up
// as the Kubernetes tooling spawning command interpreters or
// downloaders. Tune the child list to what your kubectl/kubelet
// processes legitimately spawn (normally: nothing interactive).
let SuspiciousChildren = dynamic([
    "sh", "bash", "dash", "zsh", "ksh", "curl", "wget", "nc", "ncat", "socat",
    "perl", "ruby", "python", "python3", "powershell.exe", "cmd.exe",
    "certutil.exe", "bitsadmin.exe", "mshta.exe"]);
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("kubectl", "kubectl.exe",
    "kubelet", "kubelet.exe", "kube-proxy", "kube-proxy.exe")
| where FileName in~ (SuspiciousChildren)
| extend SuspicionScore = iff(FileName in~ ("sh", "bash", "dash", "zsh",
    "ksh", "powershell.exe", "cmd.exe"), 2, 1)
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, InitiatingProcessFileName,
          InitiatingProcessCommandLine, FileName, ProcessCommandLine,
          InitiatingProcessAccountName, SuspicionScore
| order by SuspicionScore desc, TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 5: non-kubectl processes reading kubeconfig or token files --
// Stolen cluster credentials are the prize: a Secret read hands the
// attacker a kubeconfig, a Spot API token or a service-account token.
// Hunt processes other than kubectl itself reading kubeconfig files or
// service-account token paths. The legitimate readers are kubectl and
// the Kubernetes components, and they are excluded.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FolderPath has ".kube" or FileName has "kubeconfig"
    or FolderPath has "serviceaccount" or FileName has "service-account"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName !in~ ("kubectl", "kubectl.exe",
    "kubelet", "kubelet.exe", "kube-proxy", "kube-proxy.exe",
    "kube-apiserver", "kube-controller-manager", "kube-scheduler")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FolderPath, FileName, SHA256,
          ActionType, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
